1
00:00:09,410 --> 00:00:13,860
Let's get started.
In this lesson,

2
00:00:13,860 --> 00:00:16,505
we're going to talk about
the Linux Data Plan.

3
00:00:16,505 --> 00:00:20,040
As mentioned in the
previous lesson,

4
00:00:20,040 --> 00:00:22,720
we're not going to get into

5
00:00:22,720 --> 00:00:27,740
the kernel hacking and really
explore the source code.

6
00:00:27,740 --> 00:00:30,100
That would be a fun
thing to do and

7
00:00:30,100 --> 00:00:32,380
you're highly
encouraged to do that,

8
00:00:32,380 --> 00:00:36,200
but really this is meant to
just give an overview of what

9
00:00:36,200 --> 00:00:38,840
the capabilities are
so that you could

10
00:00:38,840 --> 00:00:42,180
start exploring on your
own if you wanted to.

11
00:00:42,180 --> 00:00:46,480
Really, it sets the stage
for how we're going

12
00:00:46,480 --> 00:00:48,200
to interact with Linux

13
00:00:48,200 --> 00:00:51,600
and know what its
capabilities are.

14
00:00:52,880 --> 00:00:59,190
Linux networking has a
rich set of functionality.

15
00:00:59,190 --> 00:01:04,100
It provides abilities to
do forwarding, netting,

16
00:01:04,100 --> 00:01:06,860
filtering, bridging,
load balancing,

17
00:01:06,860 --> 00:01:09,520
traffic shaping,
many other things.

18
00:01:09,520 --> 00:01:13,100
I basically just found
as many icons on

19
00:01:13,100 --> 00:01:17,255
the Internet that I could find
and through them up here.

20
00:01:17,255 --> 00:01:19,980
But really what you should

21
00:01:19,980 --> 00:01:24,620
understand is there's a lot
that you could do with Linux.

22
00:01:24,620 --> 00:01:26,510
This is part of the reason

23
00:01:26,510 --> 00:01:30,770
why there's that movement
as we mentioned,

24
00:01:30,770 --> 00:01:36,100
around desegregation and Linux
is forming the foundation.

25
00:01:36,110 --> 00:01:39,885
How does this actually
get implemented?

26
00:01:39,885 --> 00:01:42,225
All of that functionality.

27
00:01:42,225 --> 00:01:45,230
Stepping back, shown here

28
00:01:45,230 --> 00:01:48,170
is a generic router
architecture.

29
00:01:48,170 --> 00:01:50,570
On the left hand
side, you can see

30
00:01:50,570 --> 00:01:53,855
that that's the packet arriving.

31
00:01:53,855 --> 00:01:57,780
The router, we'll
do some processing.

32
00:01:57,780 --> 00:02:02,670
This is largely focused
on IP forwarding.

33
00:02:02,670 --> 00:02:05,390
It'll look up the IP address,

34
00:02:05,390 --> 00:02:09,030
it'll send that to
a forwarding table

35
00:02:09,030 --> 00:02:15,470
which the value that was
stored is the next top.

36
00:02:15,470 --> 00:02:18,410
Then you'll go through
some packet processing

37
00:02:18,410 --> 00:02:20,175
where you'll update the header.

38
00:02:20,175 --> 00:02:22,590
In IP forwarding,
you'll do things like

39
00:02:22,590 --> 00:02:26,390
decrementing the time to
live field the packets,

40
00:02:26,390 --> 00:02:27,990
then get queued up for

41
00:02:27,990 --> 00:02:34,610
transmission to one of the
particular outgoing ports.

42
00:02:34,610 --> 00:02:37,750
There's packet buffers that

43
00:02:37,750 --> 00:02:41,165
can store that, then
it's transmitted.

44
00:02:41,165 --> 00:02:44,070
This really generalizes to

45
00:02:44,070 --> 00:02:46,520
really any functionality that
you're going to perform.

46
00:02:46,520 --> 00:02:51,020
The key takeaway here is that
there's this pipeline of

47
00:02:51,020 --> 00:02:56,385
functions and then there's
tables driving the processing.

48
00:02:56,385 --> 00:03:01,260
Both of that is
equally true in Linux

49
00:03:01,260 --> 00:03:04,890
and one of the things

50
00:03:04,890 --> 00:03:08,460
to think about is
also as part of that,

51
00:03:08,460 --> 00:03:13,440
when you're providing
broader functionality than

52
00:03:13,440 --> 00:03:17,220
just the IP forwarding
is you're going to have

53
00:03:17,220 --> 00:03:21,480
to do a lot of protocol
header parsing.

54
00:03:21,480 --> 00:03:24,400
Shown here are part of

55
00:03:24,400 --> 00:03:27,530
these protocol headers or
examples of protocol headers,

56
00:03:27,530 --> 00:03:30,515
so we're showing
Ethernet and IPv4,

57
00:03:30,515 --> 00:03:36,725
and in each of the cases
there's a type field.

58
00:03:36,725 --> 00:03:43,140
In each case the type
and it's protocol here,

59
00:03:43,140 --> 00:03:45,160
the type could be,

60
00:03:45,160 --> 00:03:49,200
so this is saying Ethernet
packet and then the next type,

61
00:03:49,200 --> 00:03:53,080
whatever this data is
inside of it could be IPv4,

62
00:03:53,080 --> 00:03:54,655
could be IPv6,

63
00:03:54,655 --> 00:03:56,545
it could be an ARP packet,

64
00:03:56,545 --> 00:03:59,300
VLAN whatever it is.

65
00:03:59,300 --> 00:04:01,775
Then likewise with IPv4,

66
00:04:01,775 --> 00:04:03,860
it has something
that indicates what

67
00:04:03,860 --> 00:04:07,550
the actual data of
that packet is.

68
00:04:07,550 --> 00:04:10,445
ICMP, TCP, so forth.

69
00:04:10,445 --> 00:04:14,660
This is valuable because
this is really helps

70
00:04:14,660 --> 00:04:17,340
guide how that pipeline

71
00:04:17,340 --> 00:04:20,110
progresses as you're
parsing the headers.

72
00:04:20,110 --> 00:04:24,345
A simplified version of
the Linux Data Plane.

73
00:04:24,345 --> 00:04:26,220
Again, this doesn't correlate

74
00:04:26,220 --> 00:04:29,340
exactly to specific functions,

75
00:04:29,340 --> 00:04:31,875
but if you were to go look,

76
00:04:31,875 --> 00:04:35,130
you would see
similar things that

77
00:04:35,130 --> 00:04:36,615
may be broken up into

78
00:04:36,615 --> 00:04:38,670
different functions and
multiple functions,

79
00:04:38,670 --> 00:04:45,525
but it'll have a flavor of
this inside of their kernel.

80
00:04:45,525 --> 00:04:48,870
You start with the
device drivers

81
00:04:48,870 --> 00:04:51,120
that are able to
communicate with

82
00:04:51,120 --> 00:04:57,180
the various network cards
that would be on the device.

83
00:04:57,190 --> 00:05:01,010
It starts with a
receive function

84
00:05:01,010 --> 00:05:03,140
so it's going to be
doing some parsing

85
00:05:03,140 --> 00:05:09,415
of the headers to see what
type the packets are.

86
00:05:09,415 --> 00:05:11,940
Are they ARP? Is it VLAN?

87
00:05:11,940 --> 00:05:14,685
Is this attached to a bridge?

88
00:05:14,685 --> 00:05:16,260
Is this IPv4?

89
00:05:16,260 --> 00:05:18,150
All that kind of processing.

90
00:05:18,150 --> 00:05:24,150
For local traffic, it's going
to look if there's TCP,

91
00:05:24,150 --> 00:05:29,290
if it's an ICMP packet, IPsec.

92
00:05:29,290 --> 00:05:33,770
If it's not local, it'll
be able to be forwarded.

93
00:05:33,770 --> 00:05:35,330
There's a lot of
functionality inside

94
00:05:35,330 --> 00:05:41,130
the Linux kernel
to do forwarding.

95
00:05:41,360 --> 00:05:44,310
It's missing a lot of details

96
00:05:44,310 --> 00:05:47,920
around how's bridging handled
and things like that.

97
00:05:47,930 --> 00:05:50,310
Then we're going to see that it

98
00:05:50,310 --> 00:05:52,455
goes into queuing discipline.

99
00:05:52,455 --> 00:05:56,790
This is part of that packet
buffering and picking

100
00:05:56,790 --> 00:06:02,850
what packets to transmit next
and all that kind of stuff.

101
00:06:02,850 --> 00:06:07,080
It may need ARP on
the egress side.

102
00:06:07,080 --> 00:06:10,755
In case you don't
know next hops,

103
00:06:10,755 --> 00:06:13,125
MAC address, things like that.

104
00:06:13,125 --> 00:06:15,405
Then it gets into a
transmit function

105
00:06:15,405 --> 00:06:18,280
and ultimately gets
down to the driver.

106
00:06:18,470 --> 00:06:21,300
Throughout the Linux kernel,

107
00:06:21,300 --> 00:06:26,010
there's a lot of hook
points. We'll see this.

108
00:06:26,010 --> 00:06:29,310
These are hook points
for various net,

109
00:06:29,310 --> 00:06:32,295
filtering, and different
logic like that.

110
00:06:32,295 --> 00:06:37,720
We'll see that in a future
module that we'll cover.

111
00:06:37,970 --> 00:06:40,800
The takeaway here
is that there is

112
00:06:40,800 --> 00:06:44,100
this rich set of functionality
provided by Linux.

113
00:06:44,100 --> 00:06:46,710
It's very powerful if you know

114
00:06:46,710 --> 00:06:49,980
how to use it which is
where this course comes in.

115
00:06:49,980 --> 00:06:54,795
Now some of the sample
utilities that are

116
00:06:54,795 --> 00:06:57,540
quite useful and we'll

117
00:06:57,540 --> 00:06:59,745
go through every
single one of these;

118
00:06:59,745 --> 00:07:05,100
iproute2 which is really
a suite of utilities,

119
00:07:05,100 --> 00:07:09,630
iptables, tc for traffic
control, and ipvs.

120
00:07:09,630 --> 00:07:11,730
You can see that it
covers everything from

121
00:07:11,730 --> 00:07:13,950
load balancing, the queuing,

122
00:07:13,950 --> 00:07:17,220
classification and
filtering, and bridging,

123
00:07:17,220 --> 00:07:19,275
forwarding and all
that kind of stuff.

124
00:07:19,275 --> 00:07:21,330
Now the question is,

125
00:07:21,330 --> 00:07:26,595
how do these utilities
interact with Linux?

126
00:07:26,595 --> 00:07:30,765
This is where a protocol
called Netlink comes in.

127
00:07:30,765 --> 00:07:34,560
This is how these utilities

128
00:07:34,560 --> 00:07:37,320
tell the kernel how it
should process the traffic.

129
00:07:37,320 --> 00:07:38,850
If you recall in

130
00:07:38,850 --> 00:07:41,475
the generic router
architecture, there's a lot of,

131
00:07:41,475 --> 00:07:43,680
it was a table-driven
architecture,

132
00:07:43,680 --> 00:07:45,720
so there was a lot
of functionality and

133
00:07:45,720 --> 00:07:48,390
then there's these
various tables.

134
00:07:48,390 --> 00:07:52,080
A lot of this is just
filling in tables.

135
00:07:52,080 --> 00:07:55,275
Let's say we have a
utility like iproute2.

136
00:07:55,275 --> 00:07:57,915
It's going to talk to
the net link protocol

137
00:07:57,915 --> 00:08:02,680
to the kernel which
is all the data plan.

138
00:08:02,870 --> 00:08:08,655
How it works is it's based
off of Berkeley sockets.

139
00:08:08,655 --> 00:08:12,330
Berkeley sockets as a refresher

140
00:08:12,330 --> 00:08:15,180
from network systems course

141
00:08:15,180 --> 00:08:17,800
or network foundations course,

142
00:08:18,170 --> 00:08:23,715
sockets originated
in the early 1980s

143
00:08:23,715 --> 00:08:25,845
and it's really been
the standard since.

144
00:08:25,845 --> 00:08:29,700
All a socket is, is an
abstract representation for

145
00:08:29,700 --> 00:08:33,585
the local endpoint of a
network communication path.

146
00:08:33,585 --> 00:08:36,390
An application puts data into

147
00:08:36,390 --> 00:08:38,100
the socket and then

148
00:08:38,100 --> 00:08:42,220
the other application gets
data from the socket.

149
00:08:44,060 --> 00:08:48,170
If you were to do some
socket programming,

150
00:08:48,170 --> 00:08:50,690
you would write some
code that basically

151
00:08:50,690 --> 00:08:54,965
has this sequence
of function calls.

152
00:08:54,965 --> 00:08:56,705
On the server side,

153
00:08:56,705 --> 00:08:58,570
you would create the socket,

154
00:08:58,570 --> 00:09:01,130
create the file
descriptor and all that.

155
00:09:01,130 --> 00:09:04,230
You would bind it to a well
known port and then you would

156
00:09:04,230 --> 00:09:08,450
start listening for
incoming connections.

157
00:09:08,450 --> 00:09:11,450
You would block on
that after you call

158
00:09:11,450 --> 00:09:13,470
accept until you get

159
00:09:13,470 --> 00:09:16,150
some connection request
from the client.

160
00:09:16,150 --> 00:09:17,800
On the client side,

161
00:09:17,800 --> 00:09:19,405
they would do something similar,

162
00:09:19,405 --> 00:09:20,890
create a socket and then call

163
00:09:20,890 --> 00:09:25,075
connect to do the
connection establishment.

164
00:09:25,075 --> 00:09:26,965
Then once you have that,

165
00:09:26,965 --> 00:09:32,065
it's going to be this sequence
of sends and receive.

166
00:09:32,065 --> 00:09:33,955
This is going to be dependent

167
00:09:33,955 --> 00:09:37,430
on the application protocol.

168
00:09:37,770 --> 00:09:42,610
Looking at this from the
Netlink perspective,

169
00:09:42,610 --> 00:09:47,545
all that really is is
opening sockets where

170
00:09:47,545 --> 00:09:51,895
each application is the client

171
00:09:51,895 --> 00:09:55,855
and then the servers
run down in the kernel,

172
00:09:55,855 --> 00:09:58,120
and then you create the socket

173
00:09:58,120 --> 00:10:00,280
between the application
and user space

174
00:10:00,280 --> 00:10:04,615
and the module running
in the kernel.

175
00:10:04,615 --> 00:10:06,325
It supports multicast.

176
00:10:06,325 --> 00:10:09,160
What's shown here is
that really a lot of

177
00:10:09,160 --> 00:10:13,810
that logic is going to
be hid in by libraries.

178
00:10:13,810 --> 00:10:21,280
But just to go directly to
what the socket looks like,

179
00:10:21,280 --> 00:10:25,135
looking at these functions,
we have the socket call.

180
00:10:25,135 --> 00:10:29,170
Some of the parameters
that you would see,

181
00:10:29,170 --> 00:10:32,450
and this is all showing C code.

182
00:10:32,550 --> 00:10:37,210
The domain, you would
say it's AF Netlink.

183
00:10:37,210 --> 00:10:39,880
The type would be a raw socket,

184
00:10:39,880 --> 00:10:43,120
the protocol would
be the family.

185
00:10:43,120 --> 00:10:46,660
If you were creating
a TCP socket, well,

186
00:10:46,660 --> 00:10:50,620
you would just have
the exact same call

187
00:10:50,620 --> 00:10:53,635
with different values filled in.

188
00:10:53,635 --> 00:10:57,850
Example. Protocol values for

189
00:10:57,850 --> 00:10:59,680
the families would
be things like

190
00:10:59,680 --> 00:11:02,125
Netlink route,
Netlink net filter.

191
00:11:02,125 --> 00:11:03,940
This is really just indicating

192
00:11:03,940 --> 00:11:06,970
which particular
subsystem inside

193
00:11:06,970 --> 00:11:09,970
of the kernel that
you're talking to.

194
00:11:09,970 --> 00:11:18,565
The structure of the socket
is part of the bind call.

195
00:11:18,565 --> 00:11:21,955
You can see calls like that.

196
00:11:21,955 --> 00:11:26,320
Each family has a set
of multicast groups,

197
00:11:26,320 --> 00:11:29,875
the RTM group link,

198
00:11:29,875 --> 00:11:34,165
IPV4, IFADDR, and so forth.

199
00:11:34,165 --> 00:11:39,760
Now when you comes to
sending messages, again,

200
00:11:39,760 --> 00:11:46,180
it's taking that same generic
or same function structure.

201
00:11:46,180 --> 00:11:49,210
The data just happens to be in

202
00:11:49,210 --> 00:11:53,440
the particular Netlink
message protocol.

203
00:11:53,440 --> 00:11:56,740
It starts with a header where it

204
00:11:56,740 --> 00:12:00,460
will have the length
and type and all that.

205
00:12:00,460 --> 00:12:02,920
Where the type would be things

206
00:12:02,920 --> 00:12:07,840
like what are you trying
to do with this message?

207
00:12:07,840 --> 00:12:11,485
What is the payload
going to indicate?

208
00:12:11,485 --> 00:12:13,750
In this case, it's, say,

209
00:12:13,750 --> 00:12:15,920
setting up a new link.

210
00:12:16,830 --> 00:12:21,040
Then the payload
would have a bunch of

211
00:12:21,040 --> 00:12:25,630
other information
that's specific

212
00:12:25,630 --> 00:12:28,700
to the request
that's being made.

213
00:12:29,190 --> 00:12:34,300
All of that isn't really to
make you net link experts,

214
00:12:34,300 --> 00:12:37,330
I'll provide references if

215
00:12:37,330 --> 00:12:39,280
you do want to go and
actually try to create

216
00:12:39,280 --> 00:12:41,830
some Netlink messages with

217
00:12:41,830 --> 00:12:44,980
the kernel and explore
that, that's great.

218
00:12:44,980 --> 00:12:49,315
But the main takeaway
is, at the data plane,

219
00:12:49,315 --> 00:12:51,955
we have functions with

220
00:12:51,955 --> 00:12:55,090
tables that are
organized in a pipeline.

221
00:12:55,090 --> 00:12:59,080
There's really this
API for each function,

222
00:12:59,080 --> 00:13:04,420
then the function and the table,

223
00:13:04,420 --> 00:13:07,735
and those are exposed
through Netlink sockets.

224
00:13:07,735 --> 00:13:09,910
Then we have utilities

225
00:13:09,910 --> 00:13:11,440
that really provide
abstractions for

226
00:13:11,440 --> 00:13:14,260
users to configure
various aspects

227
00:13:14,260 --> 00:13:15,730
of the Linux data plane.

228
00:13:15,730 --> 00:13:18,310
It uses those APIs provided by

229
00:13:18,310 --> 00:13:19,390
the data plane through

230
00:13:19,390 --> 00:13:21,940
the Netlink sockets
to configure it,

231
00:13:21,940 --> 00:13:28,250
but it presents as a much
easier interface to work with.

232
00:13:28,260 --> 00:13:32,350
With that, we'll end

233
00:13:32,350 --> 00:13:36,740
here and we will see
you next time. Thanks.